Whether this trade-off is favorable depends on the program
timeline and cost constraints; the Pareto frontier identifies
the set of solutions that are not improvable without accepting
a regression on at least one objective.

\subsection{Cost Sensitivity and Vehicle Economics}

Total program cost spans a
{{ "%.1f"|format(max_cost / min_cost) if min_cost > 0 else '---' }}$\times$
range from
\${{ "%.0f"|format(min_cost) }}\,M to
\${{ "%.0f"|format(max_cost) }}\,M across the ensemble.
This range is explained primarily by vehicle selection:
the optimizer's choice between high-unit-cost vehicles
(SLS Block~2 at \$4{,}100\,M, Orion at \$600\,M/rotation)
and cost-efficient alternatives (New Glenn at \$90\,M,
Crew Dragon at \$220\,M/rotation) dominates the program budget.
First-flight cost premiums (Eq.~\ref{eq:firstflight}) add
a further fixed overhead for each unique vehicle type
introduced; scenarios that reuse a smaller set of vehicles
benefit from amortizing this premium across multiple flights.
The $w_3$-dominant scenario in the ensemble identifies the
minimum-cost architecture; comparing its vehicle mix against
the launch-count-optimal scenario reveals which substitutions
are economically efficient and which introduce unacceptable
schedule risk.

\subsection{Proximity Risk across Scenarios}

Cumulative risk (Eq.~\ref{eq:risk}) scales with the square of
simultaneous vehicle count, so scenarios that compress launches
into overlapping windows to minimize campaign duration incur
disproportionately higher risk.
Across this ensemble, the scenario with the shortest duration
({{ min_periods }}~periods) accumulated a risk of
{{ "%.4f"|format(max_risk) }}, while the
{{ max_periods }}-period scenario accumulated
{{ "%.4f"|format(min_risk) }}.
This inverse relationship between schedule aggressiveness and
safety margin is captured by the congestion penalty
$\pi(n,p)$ in Eq.~(\ref{eq:penalty}), which reduces assembly
throughput when vehicle density is high---providing an automatic
but partial economic disincentive against highly concurrent
operations.
The residual risk metric $\mathcal{R}$ exposes the component not
captured by $\pi$ and could be promoted to an explicit fourth
weighted objective in future work.

\subsection{Practical Recommendations}

The solution-space chart (Fig.~\ref{fig:solution_space}) and
Pareto frontier (Fig.~\ref{fig:pareto}) together identify the
actionable operating points for mission planners.
Pareto-optimal solutions at the ``knee'' of the frontier---where
the marginal launch-count increase required to achieve a further
duration reduction becomes large---offer the best combined
performance; solutions at the extremes of the frontier represent
specialised architectures (minimum-launch vs.\ minimum-duration)
that are appropriate only when one objective dominates absolutely.
For a balanced program with moderate budget constraints and a
multi-year assembly timeline, the cost-optimal Pareto point
(minimum \${{ "%.0f"|format(min_cost) }}\,M) likely provides
the most defensible architecture: it avoids SLS Block~2's
\$4{,}100\,M per-flight cost while remaining within the
achievable duration range set by vehicle lead times and
assembly rates.
Schedule-minimal operating points are justified only when an
external deadline (such as a planetary departure window)
imposes a hard upper bound on campaign duration.

